WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.410 --> 00:00:02.021
<v Examiner>Two marks.

2
00:00:02.021 --> 00:00:07.341
<v Examiner>Explain how a beta blocker works, and identify what the nurse checks before giving one.

3
00:00:07.791 --> 00:00:09.810
A beta blocker is an antagonist.

4
00:00:09.810 --> 00:00:15.301
It binds to the beta-1 receptors in the heart and blocks the body's own adrenaline from

5
00:00:15.301 --> 00:00:19.213
binding, so the heart rate slows and the blood pressure drops.

6
00:00:19.213 --> 00:00:23.568
They're used for high blood pressure, angina and atrial fibrillation.

7
00:00:23.568 --> 00:00:27.417
You can spot them by name: they end in olol, like metoprolol.

8
00:00:27.867 --> 00:00:32.771
Before giving it, check the pulse, apical or radial, and the blood pressure.

9
00:00:32.771 --> 00:00:35.998
If the pulse is slow or the blood pressure is low,

10
00:00:35.998 --> 00:00:38.773
withhold it and tell the R N or prescriber.

11
00:00:38.773 --> 00:00:42.064
Two more facts: don't stop a beta blocker suddenly,

12
00:00:42.064 --> 00:00:46.968
because that causes rebound hypertension; and use it with caution in asthma,

13
00:00:46.968 --> 00:00:52.388
because blocking beta receptors can tighten the airways, the opposite of salbutamol.

14
00:00:52.838 --> 00:00:53.492
<v Examiner>Two marks.

15
00:00:53.492 --> 00:00:57.873
<v Examiner>Describe G T N: its class, why it's sublingual, and what you teach.

16
00:00:58.323 --> 00:01:02.910
G T N, glyceryl trinitrate, is a nitrate vasodilator, an anti-anginal.

17
00:01:02.910 --> 00:01:07.628
It widens the coronary arteries so more oxygen reaches the heart muscle.

18
00:01:07.628 --> 00:01:11.953
It's given sublingually, under the tongue, as a spray or a tablet,

19
00:01:11.953 --> 00:01:15.885
because that route is fast and goes straight into the blood,

20
00:01:15.885 --> 00:01:17.917
avoiding first-pass metabolism.

21
00:01:17.917 --> 00:01:22.701
What to teach: sit down before you use it, because it can make you dizzy;

22
00:01:22.701 --> 00:01:26.829
it commonly causes a headache, flushing and low blood pressure;

23
00:01:26.829 --> 00:01:31.547
and if the chest pain doesn't settle as the plan says, call one one one.

24
00:01:31.997 --> 00:01:37.721
Beta blockers end in olol, like metoprolol: antagonists at beta-1, slow the heart,

25
00:01:37.721 --> 00:01:39.187
lower blood pressure.

26
00:01:39.187 --> 00:01:42.957
Check pulse and blood pressure first; withhold if low.

27
00:01:42.957 --> 00:01:44.353
Don't stop suddenly.

28
00:01:44.353 --> 00:01:50.426
G T N: nitrate vasodilator, anti-anginal; sublingual for speed and to avoid first-pass;

29
00:01:50.426 --> 00:01:54.824
sit down, expect a headache; call one one one if pain persists.
